Traditional Threading:
```java
Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Running in thread: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
});
t.start();
```

ExecutorService (Thread Pools):
Manages a pool of threads to avoid the overhead of thread creation.
```java
ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
Future<String> future = executor.submit(() -> {
    Thread.sleep(1000);
    return "Result";
});
// future.get() blocks until result is ready
```

CompletableFuture (Java 8+):
Non-blocking, chainable asynchronous programming.
```java
CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchUserData())
    .thenApply(user -> processData(user))
    .thenAccept(result -> System.out.println("Done: " + result))
    .exceptionally(ex -> {
        ex.printStackTrace();
        return null;
    });
```

Virtual Threads (Java 21+ Project Loom):
Lightweight threads managed by the JVM, not the OS. You can spawn millions of them. They map many-to-one to OS threads. When a virtual thread blocks (e.g., I/O), the JVM unmounts it and runs another on the carrier OS thread.
```java
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    IntStream.range(0, 1_000_000).forEach(i -> {
        executor.submit(() -> {
            Thread.sleep(Duration.ofSeconds(1)); // Does not block the OS thread!
            return i;
        });
    });
} // Blocks here until all million virtual threads complete
```

